{
  "dom_id": "step-q236969",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=4x.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 4x & ={360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x={90}^{∘}\\cdot n,$ where $n$ is any integer.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
